{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "tr-04-passing-people-runners-cyclists",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Trains Passing People, Runners, and Cyclists",
    "description": "Use relative speed when the object being passed is moving, subtracting for the same direction and adding for the opposite direction.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build relative speed from same-direction subtraction and opposite-direction addition, then reverse the model to find train speed, add initial gaps and cycle length, move to passenger observations, and finish with a two-walker system.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 20, Problems on Trains. Approved scope: Teach moving-person crossings with relative speed. In the same direction use train speed minus person speed; in opposite directions use the sum. Cover finding crossing time, train speed, and train length; a jogger with an initial lead; a cyclist whose cycle length slightly increases the distance; a passenger in one train observing an opposite train; and the two-walker pattern where one train overtakes walkers at different speeds in different times, allowing train speed and length to be inferred."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A person on the track turns a normal train question into a relative-speed question.\n\nThe distance is still easy — usually one train-length. The only decision is whether the train is gaining on the person or rushing toward them.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A moving person changes the speed, not the distance. A train still has to gain one full train-length to pass a person.\n\nIf both move in the same direction, the train gains slowly: relative speed = train speed − person speed.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Example: a 200 m train at 72 km/hr passes a runner at 12 km/hr.\nRelative speed = 60 km/hr = 50/3 m/s.\nTime = 200 ÷ (50/3) = 12 s.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
